## Overview

Tail-sitter eVTOL aircraft offer an appealing combination of mechanical simplicity and aerodynamic efficiency —
by pitching the entire airframe between orientations rather than tilting rotors or carrying dedicated lift hardware,
they avoid much of the complexity common to other eVTOL designs. The tradeoff is a difficult control problem: 
the stability requirements for vertical and horizontal flight conflict, making the transition between them a persistent challenge.
This project investigates whether shifting the center of mass during transition can improve static stability and controllability,
using SolidWorks modeling, CFD analysis, and a linear parameter-varying (LPV) flight model to evaluate the concept across a range of center-of-mass positions.

## Methods

- SolidWorks modeling characterizes the inertial properties of a tail-sitter eVTOL across a range of aft-to-forward center-of-mass positions.
- Computational fluid dynamics (CFD) determines the aerodynamic forces and moments acting on the airframe across those same configurations.
- Linear flight models developed at discrete center-of-mass positions are combined into a linear parameter-varying (LPV) framework to simulate 
and evaluate controllability through a full vertical-to-horizontal flight transition.


